INT. RIDESHARE CAR - MOVING - NIGHT

2:07 AM on the dashboard clock. DEV PATEL, 30s, a driver with four stars and a lanyard from his real job still around his neck, steers through empty wet streets while a HOODIE GUY in the back seat hugs a gym bag, and a woman in the passenger seat, NADIA, 20s, who ordered the ride and keeps checking her phone, says nothing, and the app chirps, and Dev smiles at the mirror like a man who has read a lot of tips about conversation.

                    DEV
Big night? Or, early morning, I guess.

                    HOODIE GUY
Just drive.

Dev's phone, mounted on the dash, lights up with a Slack message ON SCREEN: "#ops-standup - Brenda: reminder, Q3 retro at 9. Cameras ON please."

                    DEV
(muttering)
Cameras on. At nine. Sure.

Nadia's phone rings. She answers on speaker, and a man's voice comes through, thin and furious.

                    LUKE (V.O.)
Where are you? You said you'd have it by two.

                    NADIA
We're coming. Stop calling.

                    LUKE (V.O.)
Is that him in the car? Is that the guy?

The Hoodie Guy lowers his hood. He is maybe nineteen, scared, and holding the bag as though it might go off, and the streetlights flick over all three of them in turn, orange and then dark, until Dev finally understands that this is not a ride.

                    NADIA
(to Hoodie Guy)
Show him it, Marcus.

Marcus unzips the bag. Stacks of cash, banded. Dev brakes hard, the car SLIDES, the phone clatters from its mount.

This is the last time any of them will be strangers.

                    DEV
I have a daughter. I have a performance review.

                    LUKE (V.O.)
(shouting, tinny)
Somebody say something!
